EXTRA = {
"ai-crawler-user-agents": [
 ("How to verify a bot is genuine",
  "<p>Anyone can put \"GPTBot\" or \"ClaudeBot\" in a request header, so a user agent name alone proves nothing. Before you whitelist or block traffic based on it, confirm the request really comes from the company it claims. OpenAI and Perplexity publish the IP ranges their crawlers use, and Common Crawl says CCBot now runs from dedicated IP ranges. For Googlebot and Bingbot, the standard method is a reverse DNS lookup on the requesting IP, followed by a forward lookup to confirm it resolves back.</p><p>Many CDNs and security services can do this automatically and label verified bots. If yours offers a verified-bot category, use it instead of matching names yourself.</p>"),
 ("Mistakes we see most often",
  "<ul><li><strong>Blocking every AI bot with one rule</strong>, which also removes the site from ChatGPT, Claude and Perplexity answers.</li><li><strong>Misspelled user agents</strong>, such as \"GPT-Bot\" or \"Claude-Web\", that match nothing.</li><li><strong>Relying on robots.txt while a firewall blocks the bot anyway</strong>, so the allow rule never matters.</li><li><strong>Blocking Google-Extended to stay out of AI Overviews</strong>, which it does not do.</li><li><strong>Never re-checking</strong>: crawler names and purposes change, so a rule written in 2024 may no longer do what you intended.</li></ul><p>A quarterly review of robots.txt, firewall settings and server logs catches nearly all of these.</p>")],
"geo-for-dentists": [
 ("Content ideas that answer real patient questions",
  "<p>The best dental content comes from the chair. Ask your team which questions patients ask during consultations and on the phone, then answer each one on your site. Good starting topics include: how long each treatment takes from first visit to finish, what to expect on the day, how to care for a new crown or implant, when a dental problem is an emergency, what your practice does for nervous patients, and how insurance and financing work at your practice.</p><p>Each answer should be specific to your practice, reviewed by a dentist, and linked to the matching treatment page. Content like this helps patients, gives AI tools precise passages to cite, and is very hard for competitors to copy because it reflects how you actually work.</p>")],
"geo-for-law-firms": [
 ("Common mistakes law firms make",
  "<p>The most frequent problems are thin practice-area pages that list services without explaining anything, national content with no state-specific detail, inconsistent firm names across directories after a partner change, and attorney bios that omit admissions and experience. Each one makes it harder for an AI tool to describe the firm accurately or recommend it with confidence.</p>")],
"how-ai-search-engines-choose-sources": [
 ("A quick self-check for any page",
  "<p>Before publishing, read the page as if you were an AI system building an answer. Could the first paragraph be quoted on its own and fully answer the question? Does every section heading say what the section answers? Is there at least one specific fact, number or example that a generic page would not have? Is it clear who wrote it and why they are qualified? Would a reader know which business is behind it and where it operates? If the answer to any of these is no, fix that before worrying about anything more advanced.</p>")],
"how-long-does-geo-take": [
 ("An example timeline for a new service business",
  "<p>Consider a typical case: a local service business with a new website, a small amount of content and an incomplete Google Business Profile. In the first two weeks, the priorities are confirming that Googlebot, Bingbot and the AI search crawlers can reach the site, submitting the sitemap to Google and Bing, and making the business name, address and phone number consistent everywhere. None of that produces leads on its own, but it removes the blockers.</p><p>During the first and second months, the business publishes answer-first pages for its main services and the questions customers ask most, each with a clear answer, specific details and an FAQ. The first signs of progress are usually impressions in Search Console for long, question-style queries and the occasional visit from chatgpt.com or perplexity.ai.</p><p>From the third month onward, steady publishing, new reviews and a few mentions on local and industry sites begin to compound. The business starts appearing in AI answers for specific questions about its service and area, and branded searches rise. Being named in broad \"best provider\" answers typically comes last, because it depends most on reputation built outside the website.</p>"),
 ("How to keep momentum",
  "<p>The programs that work are steady rather than intense: a few strong pages every week, monthly AI answer checks, and regular review and mention building. Stopping after an initial burst usually means competitors catch up, since AI answers are recalculated every time someone asks.</p>")],
"is-seo-dead": [
 ("A quick test for your own site",
  "<p>Open Search Console and compare the last three months with the same period a year earlier for your question-style queries. If impressions held steady while clicks fell, AI answers are likely absorbing clicks you used to get, and those pages need to become sources worth citing.</p>")],
"what-is-llm-seo": [
 ("A simple LLM SEO checklist",
  "<ul><li>AI search crawlers allowed in robots.txt and the firewall.</li><li>Site indexed in both Google and Bing.</li><li>An llms.txt file listing your most important pages.</li><li>Every key page opens with a direct, quotable answer.</li><li>Business name, services and location stated the same way everywhere.</li><li>Organization and LocalBusiness structured data where relevant.</li><li>Genuine reviews and listings on the platforms your buyers use.</li><li>A monthly record of how each assistant answers your top ten buyer questions.</li></ul><p>Work through it in order. The early items are prerequisites for everything after them.</p>")],
"what-is-query-fan-out": [
 ("An example of fan-out in action",
  "<p>Take the question \"is it worth hiring a GEO agency for a small ecommerce brand?\" A fan-out system might search for what GEO agencies do, typical GEO costs, GEO results for ecommerce, how to measure GEO, and alternatives to hiring an agency. The final answer then cites a different page for each part. A brand with clear pages on cost, ecommerce results and measurement has three chances to appear, even though none of those pages targets the original question word for word.</p>")],
"zero-click-searches": [
 ("Examples of zero-click results",
  "<p>Familiar examples include the weather box, sports scores, currency conversions, definitions, local map packs with phone numbers, and featured snippets that answer a quick question. AI Overviews extend the pattern to complex questions that used to require reading several pages, such as comparisons and how-to explanations.</p>")],
}
for _p in PAGES:
    _p["sections"] += [{"heading": h, "body_html": b} for h, b in EXTRA.get(_p["slug"], [])]

